% EMNLP 2026 Main — ARR May 2026
% Generational Depth Contamination
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}
\usepackage[section]{placeins}

\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*_{\text{empirical}}}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$---the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable---using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Across 5 model configurations (1B--7B) and 4 decoding strategies, $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Cohen's $h{\approx}0.20$), consistent across all 9 tested conditions under self-scoring (most marginal: 0.2\,pp above $\theta$): a single generation step is reliably detectable, but the second renders text statistically indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content---a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect.
Model-free statistical features enable 80--92\% cross-model transfer retention among continuation-mode generators with scorer-invariant feature ordering ($\rho > 0.90$), and cross-scorer validation confirms that $\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation process rather than the scoring model.
Both supervised classification and zero-shot frontier LLMs fail comparably at depths ${\geq}2$ (recall 14--22\% vs.\ 8--38\%), providing converging evidence consistent with $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflecting a data-intrinsic boundary rather than a method limitation.
Downstream, $\Kempirical{=}2$ correctly predicts that binary curation minimizes degradation most effectively among all tested strategies.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\clearpage
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$7B, with only one 7B configuration tested; the 7B condition uses Qwen2.5-7B-Base rather than Instruct, introducing a base-vs-instruct confound---though $\Kempirical{=}2$ consistency across this variation provides additional robustness evidence; cross-scale scoring rather than self-scoring), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; all nine conditions yield point-estimate $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$, with 8/9 confirmed by bootstrap CI---one decoding variant has 2v3 CI upper marginally at 0.601), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.002; cross-scorer validation with both Pythia and OLMo scorers drops this condition to $\Kempirical{=}1$, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}), (7)~the 15D feature set yields a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), (8)~the neural probe boundary is model-dependent ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen only; $2$ for Pythia/OLMo), likely reflecting self-familiarity rather than a universal signal, (9)~while overall feature rankings are highly consistent across models ($\rho \geq 0.85$), depth-pair-level agreement weakens at the 1v2 boundary ($\tau \approx 0.37$), indicating that per-feature contributions at the critical boundary vary across models, (10)~cross-model scoring reduces $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1 for continuation-mode data (Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}), indicating that the 1v2 boundary signal has a model-specific component consistent with the representation-dependent neural probe finding; in deployment the scorer model is known, making self-scoring the natural choice, but unknown-generator scenarios remain a limitation, (11)~all experiments use 256-token segments matching common web-scale chunking; $\Kempirical$ is defined conditionally on segment length, and longer documents with more stable per-document statistics could yield higher $\Kempirical$, and (12)~structural composition: depth~0 texts are human-authored (mean ${\approx}$191 tokens after truncation) while depth~1+ are machine-generated (mean 214--256 tokens); this length asymmetry makes 0v1 detection \emph{more conservative} (shorter texts provide less feature-extraction context), not inflated, and depths 1--5 exhibit no systematic length trend (EOS truncation rates stable across depths within each model), so the critical $\Kempirical{=}2$ finding---based on 1v2 vs.\ 2v3 comparisons---is unaffected by this confound.
Detailed analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing---positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use the publicly available C4 corpus and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}
\input{appendix_extended}

\end{document}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{The One-Wash Laundering Effect}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}

The shallowness of $\Kempirical = 2$ reflects the concentration of information loss: the 0v1 boundary is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.96$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$---the generation operator acts as a lossy channel with diminishing marginal information loss.
Consistency across four decoding strategies (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}) reinforces that $\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation process rather than the sampling algorithm.
Even at the most marginal 1v2 condition (Qwen-continuation, CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.2\,pp), $\Kempirical = 2$ survives under self-scoring; on the 2v3 side, CI upper bounds confirm non-discrimination in 8/9 conditions, with the single borderline case (nucleus $p{=}0.9$, CI upper${} = 0.601$) consistent with measurement noise at near-threshold values.
Cross-scale validation at 7B (Section~\ref{sec:kstar_boundary}) extends this from 1.5B to 7B despite reduced 0v1 signal ($0.9638$ vs.\ $> 0.99$).

The practical consequence is a three-tier taxonomy: human-authored (depth~0), first-generation synthetic (depth~1), and deeper synthetic (depth~$\geq 2$).
A single generation pass suffices to render text statistically indistinguishable from multiply recycled content---a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect that limits depth-aware corpus curation to a binary boundary.
This connects to model collapse: Shumailov et al.~\cite{shumailov2024collapse} observed that tail-distribution degradation is most acute in the earliest generations; our prompt-chain results show an analogous per-text pattern.

Per-depth analysis provides the most direct evidence that this boundary is not methodological.
OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 69--77\% depth-0 recall and 8--38\% for depths 2--4 (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).
This convergence across fundamentally different approaches---supervised statistical classification and zero-shot frontier LLMs---provides converging evidence that the saturation reflects a data-intrinsic boundary rather than a method limitation.
Binary detector confidence corroborates: despite moderate Spearman correlations ($|\rho|$ up to 0.46), 6-class accuracy is at or below chance ($\leq 0.199$ vs.\ 0.167 baseline), and Qwen-rewrite exhibits a sign reversal ($\rho > 0$), disqualifying confidence as a reliable depth proxy.

\subsection{Why Graduated Filtering Fails}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}

Since 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ for all configurations, depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ approximate random labels; graduated weighting injects noise into importance weights, explaining the monotonic improvement with $\alpha$ as it converges toward binary thresholding (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
DSIR fails for a different reason---bigram feature hashing captures domain shift but not depth-correlated distributional signatures.

\subsection{Representation Dependence of the Boundary}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}

$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).
The hierarchy $\Kempirical^{15\text{D}}{=}2 < \Kempirical^{\text{neural}} \in \{2,3\}$ reflects an interpretability--sensitivity tradeoff: learned representations recover additional depth signal sufficient to push $\Kempirical$ to 3 for one model (Qwen), but this gain is model-dependent---the 15D features trade one depth level of sensitivity for cross-model universality (9/9 conditions $\Kempirical{=}2$, 3/3 models identical), making them the preferred operational choice.
Cross-scorer validation reinforces this: alternative scorers preserve feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) but reduce the marginal Qwen-continuation boundary from $\Kempirical = 2$ to 1, while rewrite-mode $\Kempirical = 2$ is preserved---$\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation operator rather than the scoring model.

