\subsection{Token Length as a Structural Confound}
\label{sec:length_confound}

A natural concern is whether EOS truncation creates a length confound: if deeper generations are systematically shorter, token count alone could inflate depth classification AUC.
Three observations rule this out.

\emph{Reversed direction.}
Depth-0 (human) texts average ${\sim}$191 subword tokens, while generated texts average 196--256 tokens across models (Table~\ref{tab:token_length}).
Length asymmetry makes the 0v1 boundary more conservative, not inflated.

\emph{Stable depths 1--5.}
Within each model configuration, mean token count varies by fewer than 7 tokens across depths 1--5, with no monotonic decreasing trend (Table~\ref{tab:token_length}).
The critical 1v2 and 2v3 comparisons are therefore unconfounded by systematic length variation.

\emph{Zero-truncation control.}
OLMo-1B produces exactly 256 tokens at all generated depths with 0\% EOS truncation, yet $\Kempirical{=}2$ holds (1v2 $\auc = 0.615$, 2v3 $\auc = 0.550$; Table~\ref{tab:s8_cv_folds}).
This natural control demonstrates that the depth boundary is independent of length variation.

EOS truncation rates vary across models (7--16\% for Qwen-rewrite, 34--36\% for Pythia, 45--48\% for Qwen-7B) but remain stable across depths \emph{within} each model, precluding length as a within-model depth classification signal.

Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} \leq 0.021$); Graduated$_{0.9}$ is indistinguishable on HellaSwag ($p = 1.00$) but worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
